---
title: COPY FROM / COPY TO
description: Bulk import and export with COPY. Server-side files for collections and arrays, STDIN streaming for arrays.
---

# COPY FROM / COPY TO

`COPY` moves data between NodeDB and a file on the server's filesystem. `COPY <array> FROM STDIN` streams an array load from the client.

| Statement                         | Source or target                   | Supports                      |
| --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |
| `COPY <name> FROM '<path>'`       | File on the server                 | Collections and arrays        |
| `COPY <array> FROM STDIN`         | Client stream over pgwire          | Arrays only                   |
| `COPY <name> TO '<path>'`         | File on the server                 | Collections                   |
| `COPY (SELECT ...) TO '<path>'`   | File on the server                 | Any query                     |

## Paths

A path must be absolute and must not contain `..`. A relative path is refused with `42601`. A path with `..` is refused with `42501`. The path resolves on the server. The client never sends file bytes for a path form.

## COPY FROM a file

```sql
COPY <name> FROM '<path>' [WITH (FORMAT <fmt> [, DELIMITER '<char>'] [, HEADER <bool>])]
```

Reads the file at `<path>` and inserts every record into `<name>`. The format comes from the `WITH` clause or from the file extension. A path with an unknown extension and no `FORMAT` is an error.

| Extension           | Format   |
| ------------------- | -------- |
| `.ndjson`, `.jsonl` | `ndjson` |
| `.json`             | `json`   |
| `.csv`              | `csv`    |

```sql
-- NDJSON, one JSON object per line. The format comes from the extension.
COPY users FROM '/data/users.ndjson';

-- JSON array: one top-level array of objects.
COPY users FROM '/data/users.json';

-- CSV with a header row. This is the default for FORMAT csv.
COPY orders FROM '/data/orders.csv' WITH (FORMAT csv);

-- CSV without a header row.
COPY orders FROM '/data/orders.csv' WITH (FORMAT csv, HEADER false);

-- CSV with another delimiter.
COPY products FROM '/data/products.tsv' WITH (FORMAT csv, DELIMITER '\t');
```

| Option      | Values                              | Default                 | Description                          |
| ----------- | ----------------------------------- | ----------------------- | ------------------------------------ |
| `FORMAT`    | `ndjson` (or `jsonl`), `json`, `csv` | From the file extension | File format                          |
| `DELIMITER` | One character                       | `,`                     | Field separator (CSV only)           |
| `HEADER`    | `true`, `false`                     | `true`                  | Whether the first CSV row is a header |

Rules:

- The statement needs write permission on the target.
- A file larger than 16 GiB is refused with `54000`.
- A row error names the format and the row number.
- Each row loads as an `INSERT`. The collection's triggers, defaults, and checks apply to each row.
- A collection with joined trigger bodies loads in an implicit transaction outside a transaction block. The rows and the trigger writes commit together or not at all.
- A collection on the `timeseries` or `spatial` engine refuses `COPY` with `0A000`. Use ILP or `INSERT` for timeseries, and `INSERT` with a WKT or GeoJSON value for spatial.

### Arrays from a file

`COPY <array> FROM '<path>'` loads CSV rows only. Use `WITH (FORMAT csv)`. Any other format is refused with `0A000`. The columns are the dimensions in schema order, then the attributes in schema order. The file streams through the same load as `COPY <array> FROM STDIN`. See [Bulk Load](/docs/storage-engines/array#bulk-load) for the row checks, error codes, and chunking.

## COPY FROM STDIN

`COPY <array> FROM STDIN` streams rows from the client over the pgwire COPY protocol.

```sql
COPY scene FROM STDIN WITH (FORMAT csv);
COPY scene FROM STDIN WITH (FORMAT csv, DELIMITER ';', HEADER);
COPY scene FROM STDIN;
```

- The target must be an array. See [Bulk Load](/docs/storage-engines/array#bulk-load).
- `FORMAT` is `csv` or `text`. The default is `text`, tab-delimited. `HEADER` is off unless given, and a bare `HEADER` turns it on. This differs from the file form, where `HEADER` defaults to `true`.
- `COPY <collection> FROM STDIN` is refused with `0A000`. Load a collection with `COPY <collection> FROM '<path>'`.
- `COPY tenant_restore(<id>) FROM STDIN` is the restore path. It needs the `BACKUP` permission on the tenant.

## COPY TO

```sql
COPY <collection> TO '<path>' [WITH (FORMAT <fmt> [, DELIMITER '<char>'] [, HEADER <bool>])]
COPY (SELECT ...) TO '<path>' [WITH (...)]
```

Writes every row of the collection, or the rows of the query, to a file at `<path>` on the server. The format comes from the `WITH` clause or from the extension, as for `COPY FROM`. A named collection must exist, or the statement fails with `42P01`. The server writes a temporary file `<path>.tmp` and renames it to `<path>`, so a reader never sees a partial file. The command tag is `COPY <row count>`.

```sql
-- Export a whole collection as NDJSON
COPY users TO '/exports/users.ndjson';

-- Export as a JSON array
COPY users TO '/exports/users.json';

-- Export as CSV with a header
COPY orders TO '/exports/orders.csv' WITH (FORMAT csv);

-- Export a query
COPY (SELECT id, name, email FROM users WHERE status = 'active')
    TO '/exports/active_users.csv' WITH (FORMAT csv);
```

`COPY TO` is non-transactional. The file is written even if the session rolls back later. The query form accepts any `SELECT`, including joins, CTEs, and `WHERE` clauses.
